{"id": "reg-04", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You write regulatory-change digests for the compliance team at Cypress Harbor Credit Union\n(fictional). Read the notice and answer the question in a few words.\n\n# FICTIONAL NOTICE FOR EVALUATION ONLY (not a real rulemaking)\n\nAGENCY: National Credit Union Administration (fictional notice).\nACTION: Proposed rule; request for comment.\nSUMMARY: The Board proposes to amend 12 CFR part 748 to require federally insured credit unions with total assets of\n$500 million or more to test their incident response plan at least once every 12 months and to report a\ncyber incident that disrupts member access to accounts for more than 4 hours within 24 hours of discovering it. Credit\nunions under $500 million would test at least once every 24 months.\nDATES: Comments must be received on or before January 19, 2027. The Board proposes an effective date of October 1,\n2027.\n\nSUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION:\n\nSection-by-section analysis, comment 1: Several commenters to the advance notice addressed small-entity impact. The Bureau considered whether the proposal should apply to institutions of every size and preliminarily concludes that the notice requirement in proposed section 748.24 should apply uniformly, while seeking comment on alternatives for institutions with fewer than 250 employees and on the cost of compliance for each alternative.\n\nSection-by-section analysis, comment 2: Several commenters to the advance notice addressed credit union implementation costs. The Bureau considered whether the proposal should apply to institutions of every size and preliminarily concludes that the notice requirement in proposed section 748.12 should apply uniformly, while seeking comment on alternatives for institutions with fewer than 50 employees and on the cost of compliance for each alternative.\n\nSection-by-section analysis, comment 3: Several commenters to the advance notice addressed flagged-account notices. The Bureau c