{"id": "reg-12", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You write regulatory-change digests for the compliance team at Cypress Harbor Credit Union\n(fictional). Read the notice and answer the question in a few words.\n\n# FICTIONAL NOTICE FOR EVALUATION ONLY (not a real rulemaking)\n\nAGENCY: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (fictional notice).\nACTION: Final rule.\nSUMMARY: The Bureau amends Regulation Z (12 CFR part 1026) to require card issuers to send an electronic or written\nalert at least 7 days before a promotional rate on a credit card account expires, and to state the rate that will\napply afterwards. The rule applies to issuers with more than 10,000 open credit card accounts.\nDATES: This rule is effective April 1, 2027. Compliance is mandatory for promotional rates that expire on or after\nJuly 1, 2027.\n\nSUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION:\n\nSection-by-section analysis, comment 1: Several commenters to the advance notice addressed consumer notice content. The Bureau considered whether the proposal should apply to institutions of every size and preliminarily concludes that the notice requirement in proposed section 1026.34 should apply uniformly, while seeking comment on alternatives for institutions with fewer than 50 employees and on the cost of compliance for each alternative.\n\nSection-by-section analysis, comment 2: Several commenters to the advance notice addressed error-resolution timing. The Bureau considered whether the proposal should apply to institutions of every size and preliminarily concludes that the notice requirement in proposed section 1026.15 should apply uniformly, while seeking comment on alternatives for institutions with fewer than 100 employees and on the cost of compliance for each alternative.\n\nSection-by-section analysis, comment 3: Several commenters to the advance notice addressed flagged-account notices. The Bureau considered whether the proposal should apply to institutions of every size and preliminarily concludes th